Durarara!! AU Chapter 1 – Part 1 (All characters aged up to 18–19. Raira Academy is now a college.)
Curtis Nightstar stepped out of the station exit and into the thick, humid air of Ikebukuro like a man who had finally stopped holding his breath.
Six-foot-three of dark muscle wrapped in a heavy charcoal-gray hoodie, black face mask pulled high, black sunglasses, and a black Jumpman cap tugged low. The red of the city lights slid across the lenses every time he turned his head. First Nations Canadian, Northwest Territories born and raised, he still carried the quiet weight of long winters and open land even while standing in the middle of one of Tokyo’s busiest wards. The oversized hoodie made his frame look a little broader than it was—solid, dense, somewhere between Simon’s mountain and Shizuo’s lean violence. Not the biggest thing on the street. Just the one that didn’t look like it needed to prove anything.
He walked without a destination, hands in the kangaroo pocket, eyes moving. Neon. Cigarette smoke. The low rumble of trains overhead. The smell of grilled meat and wet asphalt after a light rain that had already dried. Everything was loud and fast and alive in a way the Territories never were. Curtis liked it immediately.
He turned a corner near a cluster of small restaurants and nearly walked into a wall of a man.
Seven-foot-three. Black. Built like someone had taken a heavyweight boxer and stretched him until the proportions became almost cartoonish. The man was holding a stack of flyers in one massive hand and thrusting them at passersby with the enthusiasm of someone who had never once been told “no” successfully. His Japanese was… ambitious. Loud, heavily accented, and missing several important particles.
Curtis stopped. Tilted his head slightly.
That guy’s tall. But his Japanese is pretty bad.
He didn’t feel the usual flicker of caution most people would. Size was just size. He’d grown up around men who could snap small trees. This one just happened to be louder about it.
Curtis stepped closer, voice low and calm behind the mask, the Russian rolling out easy and natural.
“Hello. I’m new here. Could I please have one of those flyers?”
The giant froze mid-offer. The flyers paused in the air. Dark eyes dropped, then climbed the full length of Curtis—hoodie, mask, sunglasses, the quiet way he stood like he belonged on any street he decided to stand on. For a second the big man’s expression shifted into something closer to curiosity than the usual automatic friendliness.
Then the grin split wide.
Simon’s answer came in thick, booming Russian, the kind that turned heads half a block away even when he wasn’t trying.
“New here? Ha! Then you came to the right place, little brother! Russia Sushi! Best sushi in Ikebukuro, maybe all of Tokyo! I am Simon. You take flyer, you come eat, I make sure they treat you good. You speak Russian? Good! Most people here only scream when I talk. You? You talk back. I like that.”
He shoved a flyer into Curtis’s hand with the careful force of a man who had learned (mostly) not to accidentally break smaller people’s wrists. The paper was slightly crumpled from the sheer size of the grip.
Simon leaned down a little, still grinning like the city itself was a joke only he was in on.
“You look like you can eat. Come later. Tell them Simon sent you. I will make sure they give you the big portion.”
Curtis glanced down at the flyer, then back up at the walking mountain. Behind the sunglasses his expression stayed unreadable, but the corner of his mouth under the mask might have twitched.
“I’ll keep it in mind,” he answered in the same language, voice still even.
Simon laughed—loud, open, completely unbothered by the looks people were throwing them—and clapped a hand the size of a dinner plate against his own chest.
“Good! Good! Welcome to Ikebukuro, new guy. Try not to get hit by trucks. Or by blondes with short tempers. Both are common here.”
He turned back to the next group of pedestrians, already launching into another round of broken Japanese and flyer distribution like the conversation had simply been a pleasant interruption in his day.
Curtis stood there a second longer, flyer in hand, the red of the city lights reflecting off his lenses. Then he tucked the paper into his pocket, adjusted the strap of the bag over his shoulder, and kept walking.
First night in Japan.
Already interesting.
Curtis lifted one hand from the pocket of the hoodie and tapped two fingers lightly against the black fabric covering the lower half of his face. The motion was small, almost casual.
Still speaking Russian, voice low and even behind the mask:
“Do I have to take this off when I come in?”
Simon’s eyes flicked to the mask, then to the sunglasses, then back to Curtis’s face like he was trying to figure out if the question was serious. For a second the big man just stared. Then that wide, booming laugh rolled out of him again, loud enough that a couple walking past jumped.
“The mask?” Simon waved one massive hand like he was shooing away a fly. “No, no, no. Keep it. Keep the glasses. Keep the hat. Keep whatever you want. Russia Sushi is not a place that cares about faces. We care about food and people who can eat. You come in looking like a secret agent or a bank robber, I don’t care. As long as you sit down and order something, you are welcome.”
He leaned in a little closer, still grinning, and lowered his voice to what was probably meant to be a confidential volume but still carried halfway down the street.
“Besides… a lot of people in this city wear masks these days. Some because of germs. Some because they don’t want to be recognized. Some because their face is just… like that. You keep yours. Nobody will bother you. And if anyone does—” Simon flexed one absurdly large arm once, casual and terrifying at the same time “—they will talk to me first.”
He straightened back up, shoved another flyer into the hands of a passing salaryman without looking, and turned his attention fully back to Curtis.
“You come tonight. Or tomorrow. Whenever. You keep the mask. I will still make sure they give you the big portion.”
Curtis gave a small nod, the black fabric of the mask shifting slightly with the movement. He didn’t smile—couldn’t really, behind the covering—but something in the set of his shoulders eased by a fraction.
“Understood,” he answered quietly in Russian.
Simon beamed like he’d just made a new friend for life and went back to shouting broken Japanese at the next group of pedestrians, already forgetting (or pretending to forget) that most of them had no idea what he was saying.
Curtis tucked his hand back into the hoodie pocket, flyer still safe inside, and started walking again. The red city lights slid across his sunglasses as he moved deeper into Ikebukuro.
First night.
And the giant already didn’t care what his face looked like.
That was fine by him.Curtis kept walking.
The neon never really ended in Ikebukuro. It just changed colours and volume as the streets narrowed or opened again. He moved through it like someone who had already decided the city could keep its secrets for one more night. The heavy gray hoodie stayed zipped, the black mask stayed up, the sunglasses stayed on. The Jumpman cap sat low. Six-foot-three of quiet dark muscle cutting through crowds that barely looked twice.
Japan felt clean in a way California never had.
Out there the demons had names and faces and debts that followed you even when you changed cities. Out here the only things chasing him were the smell of grilled meat, the rattle of trains, and the occasional curious glance at the tall foreigner who never showed his face. That was fine. He preferred it that way.
He turned down a quieter side street, following the glow of a vending machine and the distant thump of music from a club he had no intention of entering. The buildings rose higher here, older concrete mixed with newer glass. One of them—four or five storeys, no lights in the upper windows—caught his attention only because something was wrong with the silhouette on top.
A girl stood at the very edge of the roof.
Not near the edge.
On it.
Her shoes were half over empty air. Long dark hair moved a little in the night breeze. She wasn’t screaming or crying or waving anyone down. She was just standing there, perfectly still, looking down at the street like she was measuring the distance.
Curtis stopped walking.
His head tilted the smallest degree. Behind the black lenses his eyes narrowed.
“…Oh dear,” he murmured under the mask, the words quiet and almost conversational. “What is she doing?”
He didn’t shout. He didn’t run. He simply stood on the sidewalk and watched the thin figure balanced on the lip of the building, the city noise continuing around him like nothing unusual was happening at all.
For a long second the only movement was the slight sway of her hair against the dark sky.
Curtis watched for a moment longer.
There was someone else up there with her. A guy. Standing a few paces back from the edge, hands in his pockets, posture loose like this was just another conversation. Curtis couldn’t make out words from the street, but the body language was clear enough. Whatever the guy was saying, it wasn’t stopping her.
The girl—Rio—took one small step forward.
Then she jumped.
Curtis’s voice came out flat and annoyed under the mask.
“Fuck sakes.”
He moved.
One step. Two. Then he launched himself at the side of the building. The sole of his boot hit the concrete wall with a sharp, ugly crack. Spiderweb fractures exploded outward from the impact, gray dust puffing into the air as the wall took his full weight for half a second. He pushed off hard, body rising in a short, powerful arc that should have been impossible for someone his size.
He caught her in mid-air.
One arm locked around her waist, the other bracing across her back. The impact of the catch was solid but controlled. Curtis twisted in the air, took the landing on his legs, and hit the sidewalk hard enough to send a dull tremor through the pavement. He didn’t stumble. He simply straightened, still holding the girl against his chest like she weighed nothing.
Rio’s eyes were wide. Her hands had automatically grabbed the front of his hoodie. For a second neither of them spoke. The only sound was her rapid breathing and the distant traffic.
Curtis looked down at her, black lenses unreadable.
“You good?” he asked quietly.
Meanwhile, in the mouth of a nearby alley, the darkness shifted.
Black shadows that had already begun to coil and rise—ready to surge upward and intercept the fall—hesitated. Then they pulled back. The Headless Rider, already halfway out of the alley with one hand reaching for the shadows that answered her will, stopped completely. The motorcycle behind her remained silent. The empty space where a head should have been tilted slightly, as if the absence itself was watching.
Celty stayed in the dark a moment longer, observing the tall figure in the gray hoodie who had just turned a suicide attempt into a clean, almost casual save. Then the shadows fully receded. The Rider melted deeper into the alley without making a sound.
On the sidewalk, Curtis set Rio carefully on her feet, one steadying hand still at her elbow until he was sure she wouldn’t collapse.
He glanced once up at the rooftop.
The guy who had been standing with her was already gone.
Rio’s hands were still fisted in the front of his hoodie.
For a few seconds she just stared up at him—at the black mask, the dark lenses, the brim of the Jumpman cap casting a shadow over what little of his face was visible. Her breathing was still too fast. The adrenaline hadn’t finished with her yet.
When she finally spoke, her voice came out quiet and rough, like she’d scraped it against something on the way down.
“…You caught me.”
It wasn’t a question. More like she needed to say it out loud to make it real.
Curtis didn’t move. He kept one hand lightly near her elbow in case her legs decided to give out, but otherwise he stayed still, letting her keep the death-grip on his hoodie if she needed it.
Rio’s eyes flicked over him again—the height, the solid frame under the gray fabric, the way he’d come off the wall like the concrete was optional.
“Who… are you?” she asked. Her voice was steadier this time, but there was still a tremor underneath it. “People don’t just… do that. Jump off walls and catch people. Not normal people.”
She swallowed, fingers tightening for a second before she forced them to loosen.
“And that guy up there… he was just watching. He didn’t even try to stop me. You did.”
Her gaze dropped for a moment to the cracked sidewalk where Curtis had landed, then back up to the unreadable black lenses.
“Why?”
Curtis looked down at her for a second, black lenses reflecting the streetlights.
“What kind of question is that?” he said, voice low and even behind the mask. “You jumped. I wasn’t going to stand there and watch you die.”
He let that hang for a moment, then asked the part that actually mattered.
“Why did you jump?”
Rio’s fingers finally loosened their grip on his hoodie. She took a small step back, arms wrapping around herself like she was trying to hold the rest of the explanation in. Her voice came out quieter than before, almost flat.
“…It was a pact. A suicide pact. I found someone online who said they wanted to do it too. We met up on the roof. I told him why I wanted to die and he just… laughed. He laughed in my face. Said my reason was pathetic. Then he stood there and waited to see if I’d still go through with it.”
Curtis didn’t speak. He just waited.
Rio’s eyes stayed on the cracked sidewalk for a few seconds before she forced herself to keep going.
“My dad’s been cheating on my mom. For a long time. And my mom knows. She knows and she doesn’t care. They both just… keep going like nothing’s wrong. Like it’s normal. Like I’m supposed to sit at the table with them and pretend everything’s fine while the whole house feels rotten. I couldn’t stand it anymore. That was my reason.”
She gave a small, humourless breath that might have been trying to be a laugh.
“Apparently it wasn’t good enough for him.”
Curtis stayed quiet for a beat longer, the black mask and sunglasses giving nothing away. When he finally spoke, his tone hadn’t changed—still calm, still direct.
“Your reason doesn’t need his approval.”
Curtis stayed quiet for a moment after she finished speaking. The street around them kept moving—distant traffic, a vending machine humming, someone’s laughter from further down the block—but the space between the two of them felt still.
When he finally spoke, his voice remained low and even behind the mask.
“I can’t say that’s a reason to kill yourself. But your life isn’t worth throwing away because of your dad. You should move forward. Because at the end of the day, your world ends with you. Your world is shaped by you. Don’t let others shape it for you.”
Rio stared at him.
For a few seconds she didn’t say anything. Her arms were still wrapped around herself, shoulders slightly hunched, like she was waiting for the words to finish landing. 
The black lenses and mask gave her nothing to read, no expression to lean on or push against. Just the quiet weight of someone who had said exactly what he meant and was willing to stand there while she decided what to do with it.
When she answered, her voice was quieter than before.
“…You make it sound simple.”
She looked away, toward the cracked sidewalk where he had landed with her, then back up at him.
“It’s not. Not when it’s your own house. Not when the people who are supposed to… I don’t know. Care. Just keep smiling like nothing’s wrong. Like I’m the strange one for noticing.”
Her fingers tightened against her sleeves.
“I’ve been carrying that for months. And the one person I thought would understand just laughed. So standing up there felt easier than going back downstairs and pretending again.”
She exhaled, slow and shaky.
“You’re the first person who didn’t tell me I was being dramatic. Or that I should just get over it.”
Rio’s eyes searched the dark lenses again, like she was still trying to find a face behind them.
“…Who even are you?”
Curtis was quiet for a moment after her question.
Then, simply:
“I’m a wanderer.”
He didn’t offer a name. He didn’t pull down the mask or lift the sunglasses. He just stood there, solid and still, like the answer was enough for now.
When he spoke again, his voice stayed low and even, the words measured.
“Miss… what happened in your family was painful, and it makes sense you feel lost. But your life isn’t limited to their choices. You matter beyond what they did, and you deserve support, not silence.”
Rio’s shoulders tightened slightly. She didn’t interrupt.
Curtis continued, the same calm tone.
“You’ve been carrying so much hurt by yourself. Anyone would feel crushed under that. But you’re not alone right now. You’re allowed to stay, to breathe, to let someone stand with you.”
A faint breeze moved down the street, tugging at the edge of his hoodie and the ends of her hair. He didn’t look away from her.
“Your parents’ mistakes are theirs — not yours. You deserve a future that isn’t shaped by their pain. Stay long enough to see what that future can look like.”
He let the last words settle between them. No pressure. No big speech. Just the quiet weight of someone who had decided, for tonight at least, that he wasn’t going to walk away and leave her standing there alone with it.
Rio stared up at the black lenses, her arms still wrapped around herself. For a few seconds she didn’t seem to know what to do with the things he had said. Her mouth opened once, then closed again.
When she finally answered, her voice was small.
“…You really mean that.”
It wasn’t quite a question. More like she was testing the shape of the words.
Rio’s eyes stayed fixed on the black lenses for a long moment.
The street noise continued around them like nothing important had happened—cars passing, a distant train, someone laughing too loudly outside a convenience store—but the two of them stood in a small pocket of stillness. Her arms were still wrapped tightly around herself. The adrenaline from the fall was starting to drain, leaving her looking smaller than she had on the rooftop.
When she finally spoke, her voice was quiet and uneven.
“…You really mean that.”
She swallowed, then kept going before she could stop herself.
“Most people don’t. They say things because they feel like they have to. Or because they’re scared of what happens if they don’t say anything. But you… you don’t even know me. You jumped off a wall and caught me like it was nothing, and now you’re standing here telling me my life is still worth something.”
Her fingers dug into her sleeves.
“I don’t know if I believe it yet. About the future. About any of it. Everything still feels… heavy. Like if I go home I’m just going to sit in that house and listen to them pretend again. And I don’t know how many more times I can do that without it breaking something else in me.”
Rio glanced up at the rooftop where the other guy had been, then back to Curtis.
“But you didn’t laugh. You didn’t tell me I was being dramatic. You just… stayed.”
Her voice dropped even lower.
“I don’t know what I’m supposed to do next. I don’t even know your name. But for the first time in a while, I’m not sure I want to disappear tonight.”
She let out a shaky breath and looked at him properly—at the mask, the sunglasses, the quiet way he hadn’t moved away from her.
“…Is that enough for now?”
Curtis gave a small nod at her words.
“That’s good,” he said quietly. The black mask barely moved with the words. “What’s your name?”
Rio hesitated for half a second, like she was deciding whether giving it away meant something. Then she answered, voice still soft but clearer than before.
“Rio. Rio Kamichika.”
She watched him carefully after she said it, as if waiting to see whether the name would change anything—whether this tall, masked stranger would suddenly look at her differently now that she was no longer just the girl who had jumped.
Curtis didn’t react in any obvious way. He simply took the name in, the same way he seemed to take in everything else: quietly, without fuss.
“Rio,” he repeated once, like he was fixing it in place. Then, after a short pause, he added, “I’m glad you stayed.”
He didn’t offer his own name in return. Not yet. The mask and sunglasses stayed exactly where they were, and the quiet between them stretched for a moment longer while the city continued moving around the two of them.
Rio stood there for another second, still holding herself like she might fall apart if she let go.
Then she moved.
She stepped forward and wrapped her arms around Curtis’s middle, pressing her face against the soft gray fabric of his hoodie. The hug was sudden and tight, the kind that came more from leftover fear than anything practiced. Her voice came out muffled against his chest.
“…Thank you.”
Curtis went still.
For a man who had just casually cracked concrete and caught a falling girl out of the air, the simple contact seemed to short-circuit something. His arms stayed at his sides for a beat too long, shoulders slightly tense under the hoodie. The black lenses stared straight ahead over the top of her head like he wasn’t entirely sure what the correct response was supposed to be.
He hadn’t expected that.
People didn’t usually grab him. Not without warning. Not like this.
After a moment he lifted one hand and rested it lightly between her shoulder blades—not quite returning the hug, but not pushing her away either. The gesture was careful, almost awkward in its restraint.
“…You’re welcome,” he said quietly, voice still even behind the mask.
He didn’t move otherwise. He just stood there on the cracked sidewalk, letting the girl who had almost died a few minutes ago hold onto him while the city kept moving around them like nothing had changed.
